== Index demo (COLLSCAN -> IXSCAN) ==
Documents in catalogue: 42010

================ STEP 0: drop idx_subject if it exists ================
No idx_subject to drop (IndexNotFound). OK, continuing.
No idx_itemType_subject to drop (IndexNotFound). OK, continuing.

================ STEP 1: find({subject:"Computer Science"}) WITHOUT index ================
Expected winning plan: COLLSCAN (the whole collection is scanned).

----- BEFORE index (expect COLLSCAN) -----
winningPlan.stage          : COLLSCAN
input stage                : COLLSCAN
direction                  : forward
nReturned                  : 2730
totalDocsExamined          : 42010
totalKeysExamined          : 0
executionTimeMillis        : 17

================ STEP 2: createIndex({subject:1}) ================
createIndex result: "idx_subject"
Indexes now: ["_id_","idx_subject"]

================ STEP 3: find({subject:"Computer Science"}) WITH idx_subject ================
Expected winning plan: IXSCAN on idx_subject, then FETCH.

----- AFTER index (expect IXSCAN) -----
winningPlan.stage          : FETCH
input stage                : FETCH
nReturned                  : 2730
totalDocsExamined          : 2730
totalKeysExamined          : 2730
executionTimeMillis        : 5

================ STEP 4: COLLSCAN vs IXSCAN comparison ================
Metric                       | BEFORE (no idx)      | AFTER (idx_subject)
-----------------------------|----------------------|----------------------
Input stage                  | COLLSCAN             | IXSCAN              
nReturned                    | 2730                 | 2730                
totalDocsExamined            | 42010                | 2730                
totalKeysExamined            | 0                    | 2730                
executionTimeMillis          | 17                   | 5                   

Observation: the COLLSCAN run examined every document in the collection
(totalDocsExamined ≈ collection size); the IXSCAN run examined only the
matching documents (plus the index keys), which is why indexed reads are
dramatically faster as the catalogue grows past ~40,000 documents.

================ STEP 5: compound index {itemType:1, subject:1} ================
createIndex result: "idx_itemType_subject"

----- Compound-index query (expect IXSCAN on idx_itemType_subject) -----
winningPlan.stage          : FETCH
input stage                : FETCH
nReturned                  : 559
totalDocsExamined          : 559
totalKeysExamined          : 559
executionTimeMillis        : 2

This query has an equality on itemType and an equality on subject,
both of which are leading keys of idx_itemType_subject, so the planner
should use the compound index instead of falling back to idx_subject or
a COLLSCAN. This is the leftmost-prefix rule in action.

Index demo complete.
